<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-ascorbate biosynthesis I (plants, L-galactose pathway)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-882" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: ASCORBATE| (ascorbic acid, vitamin C) is a reducing agent and antioxidant biosynthesized by plants, eukaryotic algae, some bacteria and many, but not all vertebrates.|FRAME: ASCORBATE| is an essential vitamin in the diet of humans, non-human primates and certain other vertebrates that cannot biosynthesize it. These species rely on mechanisms for efficient uptake and recycling of this vitamin (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-6370|). A deficiency of vitamin C leads to the disorder scurvy. |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is used in the pharmaceutical industry for the production of vitamin supplements, cosmetics and therapeutic preparations. It is also used in the food, beverage and animal feed industries. Reviewed in |CITS: [16516325] [17222174]|. In photosynthesizing plants and eukaryotic algae |FRAME: ASCORBATE| has roles in metabolism, electron transport, the cell cycle and responses to pathogens and abiotic stress. When coupled with its redox system, the ascorbate/glutathione cycle, |FRAME: ASCORBATE| can effectively regulate cellular |FRAME: HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| levels (see pathway |FRAME: PWY-2261|) |CITS: [18460785]|. Interestingly, |FRAME: TAX-1117| do not possess any of the known enzymes for ascorbate biosynthesis, and minimize photo-oxidative stress using alternative mechanisms, such as peroxiredoxins, catalases and glutathione peroxidases, suggesting that the use of ascorbate evolved after the endosymbiotic acquisition of a cyanobacterial ancestor by a non-photosynthetic eukaryote |CITS: [23109712][25768426]|. &lt;B&gt;Ascorbate Biosynthesis in Plants&lt;/B&gt; Four different pathways for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| have been described in plants. &amp;bull; The first plant pathway was suggested in 1998 by Wheeler et al |CITS: [9620799]|. The pathway starts from |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE| and proceeds via the key intermediates |FRAME: L-Galactopyranose| and |FRAME: CPD-330| (see |FRAME: PWY-882|). &amp;bull; A second pathway, known as the L-gulose pathway, also starts with |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE|. However, that pathway proceeds via the key intermediates |FRAME: L-gulopyranose| and |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS:[12954627]| (see |FRAME: PWY4FS-11|). &amp;bull; A third pathway was described from |FRAME: TAX-3747| (strawberry). In that pathway |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is formed from |FRAME: CPD-330|, as in the first pathway. However, the pathway starts from |FRAME: D-GALACTURONATE|, and proceeds via |FRAME: CPD0-1083| |CITS:[13363799][12524550]|. Tracer and feeding studies have also shown conversion of |FRAME: CPD-23305| to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| |CITS: [Loewus63]|. (see |FRAME: PWY-8143|). &amp;bull; A fourth pathway suggested that |FRAME: MYO-INOSITOL| can also be channeled towards the production of |FRAME: ASCORBATE| via |FRAME: EC-1.13.99.1|, based on the effect of overexpressing the |FRAME: AT4G26260| gene. According to that proposal, |FRAME: GLUCURONATE| is reduced to |FRAME: L-GULONATE|, which is converted to |FRAME: ASCORBATE| via |FRAME: L-GULONO-1-4-LACTONE| |CITS: [14976233]| (see |FRAME: PWY-8142|). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Already in 1958 it was shown that |FRAME: ASCORBATE| is formed in plants by |FRAME: EC-1.3.2.3| |CITS: [13522636]|, although the role of |FRAME: CPD-330| as a physiological precursor has been disputed. A breakthrough arrived only 40 years later, when several key enzymes in the pathway were described |CITS: [9620799]|. The authors have shown that |FRAME: EC-5.1.3.18| plays a key role, converting |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE| to |FRAME: GDP-L-GALACTOSE|, and characterized |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.316|, which produces the substrate for the lacton dehydrogenase. At the time there were two missing steps in the proposed pathway, for the conversion of |FRAME: GDP-L-GALACTOSE| to |FRAME: L-Galactose|. The first of the missing steps,|FRAME: EC-3.1.3.93|, was filled in 2004 with the discovery of |FRAME: AT3G02870| |CITS: [15550539][16595667]|, and the last step, |FRAME: EC-2.7.7.69|, was filled in 2007, with the discovery of |FRAME: AT4G26850| and |FRAME: AT5G55120| |CITS: [17462988][18824398]|. A combination of genetic and biochemical approaches have demonstrated that the L-galactose pathway is the dominant biosynthetic route to L-ascorbate in many plants |CITS: [10097187][17877701]|. &lt;B&gt;Regulation of ascorbate biosynthesis:&lt;/B&gt; Ascorabte biosynthesis is regulated at multiple levels (reviewed in |CITS:[18824398]|). Jasmonates, which have been shown to increase ascorbate levels in Arabidopsis, were found to induce expression of several genes in the ascorbate biosynthesis pathway, including |FRAME: AT2G39770|, |FRAME: AT5G28840|, and |FRAME: AT4G26850|/|FRAME: AT5G55120|. In addition, |FRAME: AT4G26850|/|FRAME: AT5G55120|, |FRAME: AT3G02870|, and |FRAME: G-80696| were shown regulated by light. The enzyme activity of |FRAME: AT5G28840| was found to be under feedback inhibition. Feedback inhibition of |FRAME: AT4G26850| was indicated at the transcriptional level. Experiments with Arabidopsis cell suspension culture indicate that the rate limiting step in ascorbate biosynthesis occurs between D-mannose and L-galactose |CITS:[10517845][16413588]|, possibly the step catalyzed by |FRAME: AT5G28840| |CITS:[16413588]|.</Description>
